{"summary":"Ledger scope: the only affirmative inventor authority is input/disclosure.md and the single USER passage in input/conversation.md (which is verbatim identical to the disclosure). The one YOU passage in the conversation (the drafting agent's account of edits to the FIG. 1 and FIG. 5 briefs) is context only and was not treated as support; input/brief.md was used only for title, applicant/inventor, priority and government-support formalities.

Traced clean: every claim limitation in claims 1-20 traces to an exact disclosure passage - shell/floor/side walls/upper edge, tray on feet with the lower return passage, tray side walls spaced inward forming two side supply passages, perforations, rigid spacer frame with two opposed cassettes, central inlet opening, two downward guide ducts beside the cassettes, peripheral outlet openings aligned with the side supply passages, mating channel, depending indexing rib shifting the module to the registered position where the outlets overlap the passages, the "free of a fan, a pump and any powered air mover" negative limitation, the full circulation path, resilient feet against ledges (cl. 2, 14, 18), the gasket sealing after registration (cl. 3, 18), confronting inner faces across a central space with a downward-opening central inlet (cl. 4), cassettes removable/"fitted to the spacer frame" (cl. 5), shallow condensate channel and removable wick (cl. 6, 19), discharge openings at the lower ends of the guide ducts (cl. 7, 16), pull tab accessible with the tray removed and replacement without opening a cassette (cl. 8, 19), the indicator stem/temperature-responsive latch/spring/latched exposed position/manual reset after opening/no airflow control (cl. 9-11, 20), rib meeting the channel before the lid reaches the shell (cl. 12 - supported by "seals against the upper edge of the shell after registration"), the module-only claim 13 and channel-opens-upward claim 15, and the method sequence of claim 17. Every numbered part 10-100 means one thing, is used consistently in the Detailed Description and appears on at least one figure; the numeral table matches every brief. Section line 7-7 carries the same repeated designation in the FIG. 6 brief (both arrow ends), the FIG. 7 brief and the Brief Description, and the FIG. 6/FIG. 7 arrow-direction handedness is consistent. The three broken-line curves on FIG. 1 are designated FIG. 3, FIG. 4 and FIG. 5 in the FIG. 1 brief, in the Brief Description and in each enlarged view's brief, and the left/right (near/far) orientation of the module, the mating channel, the peripheral outlet and the discharge opening is consistent across FIGS. 1-5. The application text stays shape-neutral: no "by way of example", "any closed outline" or similar renderer-justifying language appears in the Field, Summary, Brief Description, Detailed Description, claims or abstract, and every generic outline (frame, slabs, indicator housing, feet, wick, pull tab, groove, step) is confined to the figure briefs and is marked "shown schematically". No drafting note, placeholder, open question, instruction, unresolved alternative or internal comment remains in any draft or figure file (grep over draft/ and figures/ returned nothing). Cross-reference and government-support sections read "Not applicable.", matching the brief's "No priority claim. No federally sponsored research or development." The Background contains prior-art characterizations only, cites nothing, and prior_art/INDEX.md lists no citable document, so no citation was invented; tracing those characterizations is left to the later prior-art review as instructed. The abstract is a single paragraph of about 138 words and repeats no unsupported matter.

One defect found: the FIG. 5 brief extends the side supply passage down past the product tray to the shell floor, which leaves the side supply passage and the lower return passage open to each other around the tray's lower edge and defeats the perforations as the flow boundary that the disclosure, the description and claims 1, 13 and 17 all rely on. FIG. 4, which enlarges the same interface, states the opposite (the perforations are the connection). Details and a subtractive, filing-clean fix are in the finding below. No other untraced structure, relationship, result, material, shape, position, connection, operating condition or variant was found in the description or the figure briefs.","findings":[{"severity":"major","category":"internal_logic","title":"FIG. 5 brief runs the side supply passage down past the product tray to the shell floor, bypassing the perforations that the claims make the flow boundary","where":"figures/FIG-5.md, paragraph beginning "Below the module body" (final sentence) and the following trough paragraph; compare figures/FIG-4.md ("Where the thin upright wall meets that bottom wall...") and draft/09-claims.md claim 1 / draft/08-detailed-description.md paragraph beginning "The tray side walls 26 are spaced inward"","detail":"FIG. 5 first places the product tray's bottom wall at the lower end of the tray side wall ("a thin hatched horizontal bottom wall joined to the lower end of that upright wall and extending leftward, the space beneath that bottom wall being open"), and then states that the open vertical space between the shell wall and the tray wall "runs down ... to the horizontal bottom body", i.e. continues below the level of the tray bottom wall all the way to the shell floor, with the condensate trough at its foot. Because the tray is held above the floor by the tray feet 22, those two statements together leave the side supply passage 28 and the lower return passage 24 in open communication around the lower edge of the product tray, so the perforations 30 are no longer the route between them. That contradicts FIG. 4, which enlarges the same interface and states that the side supply passage "opens through them into the space beneath the bottom wall", and it contradicts the path recited in claim 1, claim 13, claim 17 and the Detailed Description. There is no affirmative inventor passage placing the side supply passage below the product tray or at the shell floor; the disclosure defines the passages only as the space between the tray side walls and the shell side walls and defines the connection to the return passage only through the perforations. The condensate route itself is supported ("Openings at the lower ends of the guide ducts discharge condensate into the channel"), so only the words that carry the passage down past the tray need to go.","evidence":"Candidate, figures/FIG-5.md: "Below the module body, a thin hatched upright wall is drawn in section, spaced to the left of the inner surface of the upright wall body, and a thin hatched horizontal bottom wall joined to the lower end of that upright wall and extending leftward, the space beneath that bottom wall being open. ... An open vertical space runs down between the upright wall body and the thin wall to the horizontal bottom body, so that the downward opening in the module body stands above that space." and "In the upwardly facing top surface of the horizontal bottom body, at the foot of the open vertical space and below the discharge opening 76, a shallow upwardly open trough is drawn in section, wider than it is deep." Candidate, figures/FIG-4.md: "Where the thin upright wall meets that bottom wall, a series of small openings passes through the product tray, so that the side supply passage 28 opens through them into the space beneath the bottom wall. These openings are the perforations 30." Candidate, draft/09-claims.md claim 1: "enters the side supply passages, passes through the perforations, and returns through the lower return passage." Nearest source wording (input/disclosure.md; no affirmative passage extends the passage below the tray to the floor): "The product tray is suspended above the shell floor by feet so that a lower return passage remains under the tray. The tray side walls are spaced inward from the shell side walls to form two side supply passages." and "descends through the guide ducts, enters the side supply passages, passes through perforations in the product tray, and returns through the lower return passage."","fix":"Edit figures/FIG-5.md only (no application text changes; add no new structure). Replace the sentence "An open vertical space runs down between the upright wall body and the thin wall to the horizontal bottom body, so that the downward opening in the module body stands above that space." with "An open vertical space runs down between the upright wall body and the thin wall, so that the downward opening in the module body stands above that space, the small openings through the product tray being the only connection drawn between that space and the space beneath the bottom wall." In the next paragraph replace "at the foot of the open vertical space and below the discharge opening 76" with "outboard of the thin upright wall and below the discharge opening 76", leaving the rest of that sentence unchanged. FIG. 1 and FIG. 4 need no change: FIG. 1 already reads "with small openings through the tray where each gap meets the space beneath", and FIG. 4 already identifies the perforations 30 as the connection."}]}
